  "route": {
    "route_id": "sinai-military-railway",
    "name": "Sinai Military Railway (Kantara-Haifa)",
    "name_arabic": "سكة حديد سيناء العسكرية",
    "countries": "Egypt, Palestine, Israel",
    "gauge_mm": 1435,
    "year_opened": 1916,
    "era": "historic",
    "color": "#b45309",
    "corridor_confidence": "medium",
    "description": "WWI British line; the Egypt-Palestine rail link",
    "notes": "Became part of Palestine Railways in October 1920; standard gauge throughout.  [route] Built west-to-east then north as the army advanced. Sinai desert railheads (Gilban, Bir el Abd, El Mazar) have approximate/uncertain coordinates. The Beersheba branch (from Deir Sneid, 1918) is held as an off-line extra_station.  [egypt_levant_link] This is THE physical Egypt-Levant rail connection: from El Kantara EAST it crossed the Suez Canal (swing bridge ~3 miles N of Kantara, July 1918) to join the Egyptian network on the west bank — i.e. it connects to the Cairo-Port Said route at Kantara. Note El Kantara (East) here is distinct from 'Al Qantarah West' on the Cairo-Port Said line.",
    "history": "A standard-gauge (1,435 mm) military railway built by the British Egyptian Expeditionary Force during WWI as it advanced across Sinai: construction began March 1916, reaching Romani by May 1916, El Arish by January 1917 and Rafah (the Palestine border) by March 1917; it then pushed north with the army, converting captured Ottoman lines to standard gauge and reaching Haifa by the end of 1918. A Deir Sneid-Beersheba branch was added by May 1918. It borrowed ~70 locomotives from Egyptian State Railways.",
    "verification_status": "unverified",
    "source_ids": [
      "cotterell_1984",
      "levantrain_compilation",
      "levantrain_system",
      "wp_palestine_rw"
    ]
  },
